    bio: "Richard of Wallingford was born the son of a blacksmith in the Berkshire town of his name, and his origins could hardly have been more modest. Orphaned young, he was taken in and educated by the Prior of Wallingford, who recognised an exceptional mind and eventually sent him to Oxford. There he studied mathematics, astronomy, and natural philosophy, absorbing the mathematical astronomy of the Islamic world that had by then been translated into Latin, and developing an extraordinary facility for mechanical and theoretical problems. He took monastic orders, returned briefly to Oxford for further study, and in 1327 — still a young man — was elected Abbot of St Albans, one of the wealthiest and most prestigious monasteries in England.\n\nHis abbacy was turbulent almost from the start. The monks of St Albans resented the appointment of so young and so intellectually unconventional a man to rule over them, and Richard spent years fighting legal battles to establish his authority. He was simultaneously afflicted with leprosy, which progressively disfigured and debilitated him for the remaining decade of his life. Through all of this, he designed and began construction of an astronomical clock of bewildering complexity — a machine intended to display the motions of the sun, moon, and stars, predict lunar eclipses, and model the tides. His monks considered it an obscene waste of money that should have been spent on rebuilding the monastery\'s nave, and they said so loudly. Richard, characteristically, ignored them.\n\nThe clock Richard designed — known from his own detailed technical manuscripts — featured a mechanism for computing the variable speed of the moon\'s motion across the sky, a problem of genuine mathematical difficulty that required an epicyclic gear train of considerable ingenuity. Historians of technology who have studied his drawings regard this as arguably the most sophisticated mechanical device designed anywhere in Europe before the seventeenth century. Richard himself did not live to see it completed; he died of his illness in 1336, and the clock was finished by his successors working from his instructions. It was subsequently destroyed during the Reformation, leaving only the manuscripts — which survived in the St Albans library — to testify to what had been built.\n\nBeyond the clock, Richard produced significant work in mathematics, including a treatise on trigonometry called the Quadripartitum, which represented the state of the art in fourteenth-century England, and an instrument he designed called the Albion — a multi-purpose astronomical calculator capable of solving a wide range of celestial problems. He is a figure who sits badly with conventional histories of science, because he belongs wholly to neither the medieval nor the modern world. He was a monk, an abbot, a man of deep faith — and simultaneously one of the most gifted mathematical engineers of his millennium. His story is a reminder that genius does not wait for convenient historical periods in which to appear."
